Z||000000||Tigger||11-20-2001||01:31 AM||||I'm realizing a good friend has depression, a lot more than what I had thought, a lot more.  She is really depressive, very inward.  The thing is, I've never met anyone like this before and I know that doesn't help.  What's tough for me is I know there isn't much of anything I can do.<P>I've had to drag myself out of some nasty bouts of depression, we all have our times, but how do you help someone that thinks everything is hopeless and is incredibly hard on themself, like the weight of the world is their shoulders.<P>The more I try to get to know her, I mean just getting to know her, like the simple things, not even real personal things.  One time when I asked her what her favorite color was, I remember getting an an awkward pause, like she didn't want to tell me at first.  Imaginable, it makes it very tough to find common interests that both of us are comfortable sharing.<P>Well, here's the worst part of all this, some bad things were said that I have unfortunately stumbled upon, and now she doesn't even want to see me.  I've forgiven her, but she is taking everything much worse.  The more I try, the more repressive she gets.  She's basically told me to save myself and find someone else better.<P>The easy thing for me is to jump off the boat, but I'm not like that.  I care about her too much.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Sorry for the long post...  I don't know what to do.  I think the best is to give everything time and be patient.<P>||||reg||9
Z||000001||terryk||11-20-2001||01:37 AM||||Find one of her family members and get them involved so she can get some help. You might lose the friendship, but the alternatives are worse. If you can't have it all, at least you can know you helped.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||11-20-2001||01:55 AM||||I've thought about that.  Thing is I'm good friends with her parents, I've known her Dad longer than I've known her.  That's kinda how I met and got know her.  I think she's afraid what her parents may say if they find out about everything and will make things even worse.<P>It's a rock and a hard place I'm in.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||11-20-2001||02:51 AM||||I'm with Terry, get other people involved in it.  A therapist/psychologist visit every week and a little personal support from friends and family will make all the difference in the world.  The key to beating depression is to realize that there IS something you can do about it, it's not something that is unbeatable.  You can only be happy if you want to be, and in order to want to be happy, you have to figure out what it is that is depressing you and beat it.<P>Just my two cents.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||dennis_6||11-20-2001||03:04 AM||||  A little thing called prayer works wonders in times like these.  <BR>  You said you care for her.  How friend only or more.  The reason people get like this is they belive there is nothing worth living for.  If it is more you could give her something to live for.  You would need to really let her know what it is in her that you like about her.  Let her know she is special.  I don't mean telling her you like her clothes or her blue eyes.  Sometimes people need to know that they are special more than just they are attractive.  Tell her what the little things are that make you care. Let her know that you haven't met anyone quite like her before.  You could bring a joy in her life that would snap her right out of the depression, but say nothing that you don't mean or it will make the situation much worse.  Women have a way of knowing if you are sincere.  I hope the best of luck to you.  <BR>  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||11-20-2001||03:06 AM||||has there been an event that happened in her past that she hasn't told you, or has told you that is leading to her depression?<P>if she doesn't want to tell her parents, maybe it involves them in someway||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||11-20-2001||04:47 AM||||Being a person who hides behind extreme levels of sarcasm and self-denial, I'm sure I can offer sound advice.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Watch some really good movies together and let the films do the talking.  Burn her a cd with songs that make you "feel" and tell her why.  Just go driving.... Let the car drive you, and try not pry at her.  I mean, just talk about random un-related sh!t to get a reaction out of her(preferably a laugh or two.)  Walk around a park and talk about stupid and embarrassing things you did when you were younger.  That always seems to make people laugh... Like the time I burned ALL of my Star Wars action figures in my sandbox using lighter fluid matches and newspaper.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/icare.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000007||Tigger||11-20-2001||09:56 AM||||I have my own feelings about therapist and psychologists.  Almost all that I've encountered as teachers in H.S. and college were nuts.  Maybe it is better those ones were teachers and not actually practicing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Anyway, I don't know how I would react if someone told me "uh, you need help."  I don't want to, but I may have to do that.<P>Very good advice dennis, I was thinking about stuff like that last night.  She has done a lot to help people, I remember one time she told me a story of how she helped a compete stranger over the internet make it through H.S. when they were ready to drop out.  I need to key on the those things.<P>I wouldn't count out the possiblility of a past event that is leading to the depression.  I know she has been ridiculed a lot.  Sad really, lots of cruel people in the world and she probably feels I'm the same no matter how much I try to convince her I'm not.<P>GT B, lol that's pretty much what we've done so far, just talk about random stuff.  I don't think I've told her about the time when I took an erector set motor and plugged the two leads into a 110 outlet.  Lol, I jumped back 5-10 feet back and onto my bed, not from a shock but from seeing the bolt jump out of the wall.  Learned a lot about electricity that day.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  It does help.<P>Appreciate all of the suggestions, keep them coming...||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierobaby||11-20-2001||11:35 AM||||Hey Tiggs I'm sorry to hear about your friend. I know how it goes though... just be there for her... my friend is the same way and as hard as it will seem to be there for her.... It will be a good thing in the end... I promise<P>------------------<BR>:wtf: <FONT COLOR="LETTER"> AIM: Fierobaby87 :wtf:<BR>http://angelfire.com/sports/sfiero </FONT><BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||falconhulk||11-20-2001||11:37 AM||||Tigger I married a woman a lot like what you describe her as.  Its been a rough 5 years but she has finnaly started to pull out of it.  Be aware they sometimes do destructive things in the moment becasue its fun or feels good.  This gives them escape from thier inner feelings.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||hugh||11-20-2001||06:14 PM||||This may sound stupid,but if she were to read this thread and see that you really care(because what you have said was unedited)and that there are a lot of people in this world that really want to help her.She may see the light at the end of the tunnel and accept the help that you are trying to offer.Whatever you do,good luck!! ||||reg||14
Z||000011||stimpy||11-20-2001||09:27 PM||||Here's my insight. This comes from someone who has battled clinical depression for years. Don't be afraid to confront it. Tell her in no uncertain terms that she needs help. Don't place ultimatums on her, i.e. "Get help or else.", that will only emphasize her feeling of isolation. But often, clinically depressed people feel that there is something very wrong with them, but think others will see them as "faking it", or that they are otherwise not worth helping. It is important that someone tell her that she is not faking it, and she is worth saving.<P>Also, don't be afraid of making her cry. Crying for a depressed person is as healthy as crapping is to a constipated person. Deep wracking sobs is ay-oh-effin-kay. That is pure emotional release. Depression is anger turned inwards, and once that is released, she can start looking at who she is really angry with. <P>Once she realizes that it is o.k. to get help, she needs to seek it. It is good to have a support system to help her through this, and don't downplay your value to her in times like this, but ultimately she needs to want to get better. 12 step programs like Co-dependants Anonymous might be a good place to start if she can't get into a medically based program, but if things are as bad as they sound, I would urge her to go inpatient into a psychiatric hospital where she can get medications stabilized.<P>And on behalf of victims of clinical depression, I want to thank you for standing up and actually caring. Depression can get ugly, and it takes a true friend to be that support.||||reg||1
